{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And any man, etc., who eats, etc.</b> The reason for the doubling ״אִישׁ אִישׁ״ — in Torat Kohanim they darshened it, and this is their language: ״Israel״ — these are Israelites; ״a stranger (ger)״ — these are converts; ״the stranger (ha-ger)״ — to include the wives of converts; ״among them״ — to include women and slaves. If so, why is ״אִישׁ אִישׁ״ stated? Rabbi Elazar son of Rabbi Shimon said: to include the child of an Israelite woman from a convert or from a slave. End of quote. One must understand why the verse needed to specify all these details, in the same way they raised the difficulty in tractate Sukkah (28a) in that baraita which said ״the citizen (ha-ezrach)״ includes women, and this is their language: From Rabbi Yehudah said Rav Nafkha, for Rabbi Yehudah said Rav said: the verse equated woman to man for all punishments in the Torah — and they answered there that it was necessary for the addition of affliction, etc. End of quote. If so, even in what is before us one may raise the same difficulty: why did it need to include the women, etc., both the wives of Israelites and the wives of converts. Furthermore, we must grasp the intent of the Tanna's words: what is this ״child of an Israelite woman from a convert״ that the verse needed to include from ״אִישׁ אִישׁ״? If he exchanged his dignity of gerut and went to disgrace, he is a gentile — and to whom did the verse come to announce the prohibition? Moreover, the Tanna says explicitly ״child of an Israelite woman,״ which implies that he stands in his Jewishness; if so, it is obvious that he is no less than a convert, whom the verse explicitly mentions — so why was ״אִישׁ אִישׁ״ necessary?" "And the Rav, author of ״Korban Aharon,״ wrote that our version of the baraita reads ״from a gentile and from a slave,״ and it would mean the child of an Israelite woman born from a gentile or a slave — that even though his father is a gentile or a slave who were not warned about blood, etc., he is liable if he ate blood after his mother's conversion, etc. — for a gentile or slave who lies with an Israelite woman, the offspring is valid, as we say in Yevamot (Yevamot 23a): ״your son who comes, etc., is called your son,״ etc. End of quote. And it is difficult for me: since the verse called this one a child of Israel, he is a full Israelite. And if the verse is speaking of a son who did not wish to remain in his mother's Judaism, he is a sectarian (paku pelileihah), and to whom did the verse come to announce the Torah of HaShem? And if [you say it came] to force him in the matter, then whichever way you turn it: if it is in Israel's hand to force him, it is obvious that they force him just as [they would] any Israelite man who transgresses the Torah; and if it is not in our power to force him, then he is not in our domain, and if so, what does ״the verse״ come [to teach]?" "<b>And let us see</b> to say, by way of prefacing with the knowledge of the reason for this prohibition, according to what the sages enlightened our eyes in the innermost dimension of the Torah: for all the souls of the people of Israel are from a level than which there is none higher, in the sod of (Deuteronomy 32:9) ״חֵלֶק ה׳ עַמּוֹ״. They also said that although they are in the lower world and far from the exalted place, nevertheless they are attached by a thread of kindness unto the place of its root, in the sod of His saying (ibid.) ״יַעֲקֹב חֶבֶל נַחֲלָתוֹ״. And the soul of the animal is from the lowest level, whose place is ascribed to the kelipah of Nogah. Now, when a person eats the blood of an animal in which the soul departs — concerning which the punishment of karet applies — he acquires a place for the animal soul within his human soul. And the verse informs us here that one who eats blood and introduces the lesser soul into his soul, the supernal root will abominate it so that it will no longer cleave to him. And this is the statement <b>And I will set My face against the soul that eats the blood, and I will cut it off from among its people</b> — meaning, He will cut the thread attached to the root, which is the source of the totality of the souls of the people of HaShem. And this is His saying <b>from among its people (מִקֶּרֶב עַמָּהּ)</b>." "It is further known that although within the totality of the people of Israel there is one root, nevertheless they are in levels one above the other, in the sod of (Ecclesiastes 5:7) ״כִּי גָבֹהַּ מֵעַל גָבֹהַּ״ etc. And since this is so, reason would suggest that since this animal soul is not impure — for it is a pure animal permitted for consumption — therefore this soul would not produce blemish and defect except specifically in the highest degree among Israel, and the punishment that HaShem designated for this transgression applies only to native-born males and not to females. And when the verse makes any inclusion, it yields to us only one incremental level at a time, until it includes some of the levels." "Also, following another path, one may say that this effect makes such an impression as to require cutting off only in a soul that is slight in worth; but in an exalted soul, it is possible that although it makes an impression upon it, nevertheless it does not produce such an effect as to require excision. Therefore the Awesome One, profound in deed, multiplied all the inclusions and stated that both lesser and greater are subject to karet for eating the blood of the soul, and He stated inclusions for all the levels, lesser and greater." "<b>And behold,</b> before you are the rungs of holiness, one above the other: the first in holiness — male Israelites; the second — their wives, since the woman is not on the level of the man; the third — a convert who comes from an Israelite [father] and a female convert; the fourth — a convert who comes from a male convert and an Israelite woman; the fifth — a female convert as in the previous two rungs; the sixth — a convert who comes from a male convert and a female convert; the seventh — a female convert who comes from a male convert and a female convert; the eighth — the convert himself. And all these rungs are indicated in the verse: the first in holiness, Israelites — it mentioned them explicitly, and this is the statement of the Tanna of Torat Kohanim: ״ישראל״ — these are Israelites. And it mentioned converts twice, with two inclusive extensions (ribbuiim) alongside them: ״הגר הגר״. And you will find that in tractate Sukkah (28a) they expounded the ה׳ of ״האזרח״ to include their wives; you may therefore conclude that the ה׳ of ״הגר הגר״ are also inclusive extensions. Here before you are four inclusive extensions regarding converts, and they correspond to the two rungs among converts: the first is the convert born of a male convert and a female convert, and the second is the convert himself who converted; and the two inclusive extensions correspond to the wives of their respective rungs. And perhaps it is for this reason that the Tanna of Torat Kohanan was precise and said ״הגר אלו הגרים״ and did not say ״זה גר״ — for he intended the two rungs of converts as stated, and he said ״הגר״ to include the wives of the converts, meaning, through the inclusive extension of the ה׳ their wives were included. And he said <b>בתוכם</b> to include women and slaves, as the Tanna stated — meaning, Israelite women and slaves whose law is equivalent to women's. And do not find it difficult why the verse needed to include them, for is it not a fortiori from converts? And one should not say that I would have said that since they are of greater standing than a convert, it would be necessary to state that karet applies to them as well, just as we said regarding the mention of male Israelites — for after male Israelites were mentioned, all the more so women. [The answer is:] behold, had there not been the inclusive extension of ״בתוכם״ by which Scripture included Israelite women, we would not have derived from the ה׳ of ״הגר״ two female converts as we said. We now have before us six inclusive extensions, and Scripture said <b>איש איש</b> — two additional inclusive extensions, which the Tanna of Torat Kohanim expounded and said: to include the offspring of an Israelite who comes from a convert and from a slave — meaning, the offspring of an Israelite that contains Israelite seed and also comes from a convert, and this is what it means by ״מן הגר״; and the intention is that his father is an Israelite and his mother a female convert, or his father a convert and his mother an Israelite woman. And these two rungs are derived from the doubling of <b>איש איש</b>." "<b>And do not</b> find it difficult why the verse needed to include these rungs after it had already included a full convert as we said — for had there not been the inclusive extension of ״איש״ we would have interpreted the converts mentioned as those who come from an Israelite and a female convert, or from a convert and an Israelite woman. From this [we understand why] the verse first placed the more esteemed rungs among converts, those that contain a share of Israel, and afterward said ״הגר הגר״ — who are entirely converts. Here before you are eight inclusive extensions. And if you should ask: a female convert who comes from an Israelite [and a male convert] — from where [is she derived]? It may be said that since even a convert himself who converted was included, it is obvious that one should not diminish from him a female convert who comes from an Israelite and a male convert, even though she is female. And according to this approach you shall proceed in every place where Scripture makes inclusive extensions such as these." "<b>בנפש וגו׳.</b> In Torat Kohanim they expounded: ״and not for the congregation״ — thus far. The reason is that there is no power in this sin to bring about excision upon a congregation. And see what I explained above (Leviticus 17:4)." "<b>מקרב עמה.</b> In Torat Kohanim they expounded: ״and his people remains whole״ — thus far. And the intention behind this is not known. Whichever way you frame it: if they are liable by the law of guarantorship (arvut), why should they be exempt here? And if it is not speaking of one who is liable by the law of guarantorship — such as where they did not know or were coerced — then it goes without saying. It appears that the intention is to say that the excision of the bad branch from the tree improves it, as is known. Therefore it said ״מקרב עמה״ — meaning, so that the bad branch not cause harm to a shoot of His plantings, may He be blessed, He excises it, and His people remains whole."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {1 "עכו״ם resolved as עובד כוכבים ומזלות (gentile/idolater). פקו פליליה rendered as an Aramaic legal term for sectarian/apostate status, left in transliteration to preserve the Talmudic register.", 5 "The phrase 'a convert who comes from an Israelite and a female convert' in the third rung and similar formulations describe the parentage of the convert's children, i.e., someone born into conversion status through mixed Israelite-convert parentage. Throughout, Or HaChaim is cataloguing eight spiritual rungs corresponding to different lineage combinations.", 6 "The bracketed insertions clarify the implied parentage combinations that Or HaChaim refers to elliptically; I kept them minimal to preserve the original's compressed style."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:58:33.159973998Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}